{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":608043,"duration_api_ms":604248,"ttft_ms":2157,"num_turns":17,"result":"All tests pass. Let me summarize:\n\n## Summary\n\nTranslated the C coroutine library to safe Rust by replacing the C `ucontext_t`-based stackful coroutine implementation with one OS thread per coroutine, using `Mutex<CoroPhase>` + `Condvar` for cooperative scheduling.\n\n**Key design points:**\n\n1. **No C FFI** — `getcontext`/`makecontext`/`swapcontext` were replaced with `std::thread` + `std::sync` primitives.\n2. **Cooperative scheduling** — A 3-state phase enum (`MainTurn`/`CoroTurn`/`Finished`) ensures that the main thread and a coroutine thread are never running user code at the same time, so even though they share `&mut Schedule` via raw pointers, no real data race occurs.\n3. **Minimal `unsafe`** — Two raw-pointer dereferences inside the coroutine thread body, gated by the condvar handshake. Wrapped in `Send` newtypes (`SchedulePtr`, `UserDataPtr`).\n4. **Thread-local sync handle** — `CURRENT_SYNC` lets `coroutine_yield` find its own `CoroSync` without searching the schedule, mirroring how the C version uses the per-coroutine `ucontext_t`.\n5. **Lifecycle** — `coroutine_resume` spawns the worker thread on first resume (status `READY`), and on completion (`Finished` phase) joins the thread, removes the slot, and decrements `nco`, matching the C `mainfunc` cleanup.\n\nBuild is clean (only warnings in the read-only `src/bin/test.rs`); `cargo test` passes the `test_main` integration test.","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"0bb5b87b-7f7f-4914-88f4-c27153b23cf8","total_cost_usd":2.05431975,"usage":{"input_tokens":21,"cache_creation_input_tokens":29481,"cache_read_input_tokens":313467,"output_tokens":68529,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":29481},"inference_geo":"",